 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Environment, energy & disasters",
  "title": "Smog season opens: Delhi on alert, Kraków on watch",
  "body": "**Delhi.** Air regulator CAQM moved Delhi-NCR to GRAP Stage III on Nov 6, before air reached 'severe' citywide. AQI was about 365, with hotspots above 400. Stage III halts most construction, which hits daily-wage building workers hardest, and restricts older petrol and diesel cars. The Supreme Court's green-cracker window (Nov 7–8) falls on the same days IITM forecasts 'severe' air. Punjab's farm fires (357 on Nov 6) and calm winds add to the load. On Nov 6 the court said more orders alone would not fix things, named vehicles as a major source and hears the case again on Nov 16. Congress blames the BJP, which now runs the Centre, the Delhi government and the city bodies. The BJP points to stubble smoke and weather.\n\n**Kraków.** Smog season is starting there too. Calm, frosty weather pushed PM2.5 to an 'unhealthy' level early on Nov 6 (aqicn). IMGW has fog warnings out, and the same conditions are forecast through the weekend. No official GIOŚ alert has been issued yet.\n\n**The Ruczaj beavers.** A smaller environment fight is unfolding in Kraków. Developer ATAL is building a city flood-retention reservoir near ul. Obozowa under a permit based on a 2021 local plan, and that plan did not record the beaver wetland that has since formed. RDOŚ ruled on Nov 5 that earthworks broke nature-protection law. On Nov 6 Deputy Mayor Wałachowski's team confirmed machinery in the habitat and notified police. The dispute sets flood protection against a self-made wetland that may hold water better than concrete. It is an early test of whether Gibała's team will make developers redesign projects when old plans no longer match the ground.\n\n**What to watch:** Delhi AQI on Nov 8–9 and any move to Stage IV; the Nov 16 hearing; police action at Ruczaj.",
  "links": [
   {
    "label": "The Week",
